# Normalize line endings for the whole repository: check out LF for
# every text file, regardless of the checking-out client's own
# core.autocrlf setting. Windows Git's common default (autocrlf=true)
# was silently checking every shell/Python script under tools/ (and
# every vendored shell/mksh, shell/toybox source file) out with CRLF
# line endings on this project's own Windows machines -- harmless for
# anything only ever *read* by a text editor or the Windows-native
# build path, but a real, confirmed-live bug for a *shebang* script
# later executed on Linux/macOS/WSL: `#!/bin/sh\r` makes the kernel
# look for an interpreter literally named `/bin/sh` with a trailing
# carriage return, which does not exist -- `bad interpreter: No such
# file or directory`, confirmed for real (2026-08-31) trying to run
# tools/crt-cc from a Windows-git-cloned checkout via WSL's own
# /mnt/c/ path, see HISTORY.md's matching entry for the fuller trail.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-native scripts keep their native CRLF -- both the correct
# convention for these formats, and nothing on this project's own
# Linux/macOS/WSL side ever executes a .cmd/.ps1 file directly.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Known binary assets: never text-normalize these, regardless of git's
# own content-based binary heuristic (which text=auto above already
# relies on for everything not listed here) getting it right or not --
# an explicit list costs nothing and removes any doubt for exactly the
# assets a line-ending mistake would visibly and audibly break
# (bundled fonts, mksh's own icon, vendored tarballs porting recipes
# fetch-check against).
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.zip binary
*.tar binary
*.tgz binary
*.tbz2 binary
*.bz2 binary
*.gz binary
